  "live_records": {
    "q-107930": {
      "dom_id": "step-q107930",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "The second-order differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+t{y}^{′}-{t}^{3}y={e}^{t}\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t).$ Find the function $f(t).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=-t{y}^{′}+{t}^{3}y+{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=-t{x}_{2}+{t}^{3}{x}_{1}+{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={t}^{3}{x}_{1}-t{x}_{2}+{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nf(t)={t}^{3}{x}_{1}-t{x}_{2}+{e}^{t}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
