    "q-330761": {
      "dom_id": "step-q330761",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "The third-order differential equation ${y}^{‴}+{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-{e}^{2t}{y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ can be expressed as the matrix equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ where\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td rowspan=\"3\" height=\"40px\" align=\"center\">$x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,A=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"30px\"></td>\n<td width=\"30px\" align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td width=\"30px\" align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td rowspan=\"3\" height=\"40px\" align=\"center\">$\\,,\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"30px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"30px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"30px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"30px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">${e}^{2t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-5}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"30px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-6}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nand for $y=y(t),$ we have $y={x}_{1}(t),$ ${y}^{′}={x}_{2}(t),$ and ${y}^{″}={x}_{3}(t).$",
      "worked_solution": "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={y}^{′}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{‴}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-{t}^{2}{y}^{″}+{e}^{2t}{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-{t}^{2}{x}_{3}+{e}^{2t}{x}_{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={e}^{2t}{x}_{2}-{t}^{2}{x}_{3}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t} & -{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
